635	\subsection{Instruction Format}\label{sec:isa-fmt}
636	%% {{{
637	In general, ZipCPU instructions fit in one of the formats shown in
638	Fig.~\ref{fig:iset-format}.
639	\begin{figure}\begin{center}
640	\begin{bytefield}[endianness=big]{32}
641	\bitheader{0-31}\\
642	\begin{leftwordgroup}{Standard}\bitbox{1}{0}\bitbox[tlr]{4}{}
643			\bitbox[lrt]{5}{OpCode}
644			\bitbox[lrt]{3}{}
645			\bitbox{1}{0}
646			\bitbox{18}{18-bit Signed Immediate} \\
647	\bitbox{1}{0}\bitbox[lr]{4}{DR}
648			\bitbox[lrb]{5}{}
649			\bitbox[lr]{3}{Cnd}
650			\bitbox{1}{1}
651			\bitbox{4}{BR}
652			\bitbox{14}{14-bit Signed Immediate}\end{leftwordgroup} \\
653	\begin{leftwordgroup}{MOV}\bitbox{1}{0}\bitbox[lr]{4}{}
654			\bitbox[lrt]{5}{5'hd}
655			\bitbox[lrb]{3}{}
656			\bitbox{1}{A}
657			\bitbox{4}{BR}
658			\bitbox{1}{B}
659			\bitbox{13}{13-bit Signed Immediate}\end{leftwordgroup} \\
660	\begin{leftwordgroup}{LDI}\bitbox{1}{0}\bitbox[lrb]{4}{}
661			\bitbox{4}{4'hc}
662			\bitbox{23}{23-bit Signed Immediate}\end{leftwordgroup} \\
663	\begin{leftwordgroup}{NOOP}\bitbox{1}{0}\bitbox{3}{3'h7}
664			\bitbox{1}{}
665			\bitbox{2}{11}
666			\bitbox{3}{xxx}
667			\bitbox{22}{Ignored}
668			\end{leftwordgroup} \\
669	\end{bytefield}
670	\caption{Zip Instruction Set Format}\label{fig:iset-format}
671	\end{center}\end{figure}
672	The basic format is that some operation, defined by the OpCode, is applied
673	if a condition, Cnd, is true in order to produce a result which is placed in
674	the destination register (DR).  The destination register also forms the "A"
675	operand of any instruction.  The ``B'' operand is formed from either an
676	18--bit signed immediate, or a 14--bit signed immediate plus the value
677	contained within a second register.
678	
679	There are a couple of exceptions to this general instruction model.  The
680	first is the {\tt MOV} instruction, which steals bits~13 and~18 to allow
681	supervisor access to user registers.  In supervisor mode, these are set to
682	one to reference user registers, zero otherwise.  They are ignored in user
683	mode.  The second exception is the load 23--bit signed immediate instruction
684	({\tt LDI}).  This instruction accepts no conditions and uses only a 4-bit
685	opcode.  The third exception is the {\tt NOOP} instruction group, encoding the
686	{\tt BREAK}, {\tt LOCK}, {\tt SIM}, and {\tt NOOP} instructions.  These
687	instructions ignore their register and immediate settings.  Further, the
688	immediate bits used by these opcodes are available for simulation or debug
689	facilities, but otherwise ignored by the CPU.  Finally, there is an (optional)
690	compressed instruction format that we'll cover later.
691	%% }}}
692	\subsection{Instruction OpCodes}\label{sec:isa-opcodes}
693	%% {{{
694	32 possible instructions can be generated from a 5--bit opcode field.
695	Tbl.~\ref{tbl:iset-opcodes}.
696	\begin{table}\begin{center}
697	\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|l|c|} \hline \rowcolor[gray]{0.85}
698	OpCode & & A-Reg & Instruction &Sets CC \\\hline\hline
699	5'h00 & {\tt SUB} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Subtract} &   \\\cline{1-4}
700	5'h01 & {\tt AND} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Bitwise And} &   \\\cline{1-4}
701	5'h02 & {\tt ADD} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Add two numbers} &   \\\cline{1-4}
702	5'h03 & {\tt OR}  & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Bitwise Or} & Y \\\cline{1-4}
703	5'h04 & {\tt XOR} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Bitwise Exclusive Or} &   \\\cline{1-4}
704	5'h05 & {\tt LSR} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Logical Shift Right} &   \\\cline{1-4}
705	5'h06 & {\tt LSL} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Logical Shift Left} &   \\\cline{1-4}
706	5'h07 & {\tt ASR} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Arithmetic Shift Right} &   \\\hline
707	
708	5'h08 & {\tt BREV} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Bit Reverse B operand into result}&  \\\cline{1-4}
709	5'h09 & {\tt LDILO} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Load Immediate Low} & N\\\hline
710	5'h0a & {\tt MPYUHI} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Upper 32 of 64 bits from an unsigned 32x32 multiply} &  \\\cline{1-4}
711	5'h0b & {\tt MPYSHI} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Upper 32 of 64 bits from a signed 32x32 multiply} & Y \\\cline{1-4}
712	5'h0c & {\tt MPY} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Lower 32 of 64 bits from a 32x32 bit multiply} & \\\hline
713	5'h0d & {\tt MOV} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Move OpB into Ra} & N \\\hline
714	5'h0e & {\tt DIVU} & R0-R13 & Divide, unsigned & Y \\\cline{1-4}
715	5'h0f & {\tt DIVS} & R0-R13 & Divide, signed &  \\\hline\hline
716	%
717	5'h10 & {\tt CMP} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Compare (Ra-OpB) to zero} & Y \\\cline{1-4}
718	5'h11 & {\tt TST} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Test (AND w/o setting result)} &   \\\hline
719	5'h12 & {\tt LW} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Load a 32-bit word from memory (OpB) into Ra} & \\\cline{1-4}
720	5'h13 & {\tt SW} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Store a 32-bit word from Ra into memory at (OpB)} &  \\\cline{1-4}
721	5'h14 & {\tt LH} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Load 16-bits from memory (opB) into Ra, clear upper 16 bits} & N \\\cline{1-4}
722	5'h15 & {\tt SH} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Store the lower 16-bits of Ra into memory at (OpB)} &  \\\cline{1-4}
723	5'h16 & {\tt LB} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Load 8-bits from memory (OpB) into Ra, clear upper 24 bits} & \\\cline{1-4}
724	5'h17 & {\tt SB} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Store the lower 8-bits of Ra into memory at (OpB)} &  \\\hline\hline
725	5'h18/9 & {\tt LDI} & \multicolumn{2}{l|}{Load 23--bit signed immediate} & N \\\hline\hline
726	5'h1a & {\tt FPADD} & R0-R13 & (Reserved for) Floating point add &  \\\cline{1-4}
727	5'h1b & {\tt FPSUB} & R0-R13 & (Reserved for) Floating point subtract &   \\\cline{1-4}
728	5'h1c & {\tt FPMPY} & R0-R13 & (Reserved for) Floating point multiply & Y \\\cline{1-4}
729	5'h1d & {\tt FPDIV} & R0-R13 & (Reserved for) Floating point divide &   \\\cline{1-4}
730	5'h1e & {\tt FPI2F} & R0-R13 & (Reserved for) Convert integer to floating point &   \\\cline{1-4}
731	5'h1f & {\tt FPF2I} & R0-R13 & (Reserved for) Convert floating point to integer &   \\\hline\hline
732	5'h1c & {\tt BREAK} &None(15)& Debugger break point & \\\cline{1-4}
733	5'h1d & {\tt LOCK} &None(15)& Begin an atomic access sequence & N\\\cline{1-4}
734	5'h1e & {\tt SIM}  &None(15)& Simulation--only instruction &\\\cline{1-4}
735	5'h1f & {\tt NOOP} &None(15)&&\\\hline
736	\end{tabular}
737	\caption{ZipCPU OpCodes}\label{tbl:iset-opcodes}
738	\end{center}\end{table}
739	shows how these 32--values have been allocated to implement 29~instructions.
740	An additional six instruction opcodes are reserved for a (potential, future,
741	optional) single precision floating point accelerator.
742	
743	%% }}}
744	\subsection{Conditional Instructions}\label{sec:isa-cond}
745	%% {{{
746	Most, although not quite all, instructions may be conditionally executed.  
747	The 23--bit load immediate instruction, together with the special instructions,
748	{\tt NOOP}, {\tt SIM}, {\tt BREAK}, and {\tt LOCK}, are the exceptions to this
749	rule.  All other instructions may be conditionally executed.
750	
751	From the four condition code flags, eight conditions are defined, as shown in
752	Tbl.~\ref{tbl:conditions}.
753	\begin{table}\begin{center}
754	\begin{tabular}{l|l|l}
755	Code & Mnemonic & Condition \\\hline
756	3'h0 & None & Always execute the instruction \\
757	3'h1 & {\tt .Z} & Zero.  Only execute when `Z' is set \\
758	3'h2 & {\tt .LT}& Less than.  Only execute when `N' is set \\
759	3'h3 & {\tt .C} & Carry set (Also known as less-than unsigned) \\
760	3'h4 & {\tt .V} & Overflow.  Only execute when `V' is set\\
761	3'h5 & {\tt .NZ}& Not zero.  Only execute when `Z' is clear \\
762	3'h6 & {\tt .GE}& Greater than or equal.  Executes when `N' is clear\\
763	3'h7 & {\tt .NC}& Not carry (also known as greater-than or equal, unsigned) \\
764	\end{tabular}
765	\caption{Conditions for conditional operand execution}\label{tbl:conditions}
766	\end{center}\end{table}
767	There are no condition codes for either less than or equal, or for greater
768	than, whether signed or unsigned.  In a similar fashion, there is no condition
769	code for not V.  Ways of handling non--supported conditions are discussed
770	in Sec.~\ref{sec:in-mcond}.
771	
772	With the exception of \hbox{\tt CMP} and \hbox{\tt TST} instructions,
773	conditionally executed instructions will not further adjust the
774	condition codes.  This allows conditional instruction sequences to be
775	strung together.  Conditional \hbox{\tt CMP} or \hbox{\tt TST} instructions
776	will adjust conditions whenever they are executed.  In this way, multiple
777	conditions may be evaluated without branches, creating a sort of logical
778	and--but only if all the conditions are the same.  For example, to do
779	something if \hbox{\tt R0} is one and \hbox{\tt R1} is two, one might
780	implement the assembly shown in Tbl.~\ref{tbl:dbl-condition}.
781	\begin{table}\begin{center}
782	\begin{tabular}{l}
783		{\tt CMP 1,R0} \\
784		{\em ; Condition codes are now set based upon R0-1} \\
785		{\tt CMP.Z 2,R1} \\
786		{\em ; If R0 $\neq$ 1, conditions are unchanged, {\tt Z} is still false.} \\
787		{\em ; If R0 $=$ 1, conditions are now set based upon R1-2.} \\
788		{\em ; Now some instruction could be done based upon the conjunction} \\
789		{\em ; of both of these conditions.} \\
790		{\em ; While we use the example of a {\tt SW}, it could easily be any
791			other instruction.} \\
792		{\tt SW.Z R0,(R2)} \\
793	\end{tabular}
794	\caption{An example of a double conditional}\label{tbl:dbl-condition}
795	\end{center}\end{table}
796	This ability to generate double conditions is used heavily by the compiler
797	when comparing 64-bit numbers together.
798	
799	The real utility of conditionally executed instructions is that, unlike
800	conditional branches, conditionally executed instructions will not clear the
801	pipeline if they are not executed.
802	%% }}}
803	\subsection{Modifying Conditions}\label{sec:in-mcond}
804	%% {{{
805	A quick look at the list of conditions supported by the ZipCPU and listed
806	in Tbl.~\ref{tbl:conditions} reveals that the ZipCPU does not have a full set
807	of conditions.  Tbl.~\ref{tbl:creating-conditions}, therefore,
808	\begin{table}\begin{center}
809	\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}\hline
810	Unsupported condition & Modified & Name \\\hline\hline
811	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Imm,Ry\\BLE label} % If Ry <= Rx -> Ry < Rx+1
812		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP 1+Imm,Ry\\BLT label}
813		& Less-than or equal (signed, {\tt Z} or {\tt N} set)\\[4mm]\hline
814	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Rx,Ry\\BLE label} % If Ry <= Rx -> Ry < Rx+1
815		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Rx,Ry\\BLT label\\BZ label}
816		& Less-than or equal (signed, {\tt Z} or {\tt N} set)\\[4mm]\hline\hline
817	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Imm,Ry\\BGT label}	% if (Ry > Rx) -> Rx < Ry
818		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP 1+Imm,Ry\\BGE label}
819		& Greater-than (immediate) \\[4mm]\hline
