821		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Ry,Rx\\BLT label}
822		& Greater-than (register) \\[4mm]\hline\hline
823	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Imm,Ry\\BLEU label}
824		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP 1+Imm,Ry\\BC label}
825		& Less-than or equal, unsigned immediate \\[4mm]\hline
826	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Rx,Ry\\BLEU label}
827		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Ry,Rx\\BNC label}
828		& Less-than or equal unsigned register\\[4mm]\hline\hline
829	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Imm,Ry\\BGTU label}	% if (Ry > Rx) -> Rx < Ry
830		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP 1+Imm,Ry\\BNC label}
831		& Greater-than unsigned (immediate)\\[4mm]\hline
832	\parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Rx,Ry\\BGTU label}	% if (Ry > Rx) -> Rx < Ry
833		& \parbox[t]{1.5in}{\tt CMP Ry,Rx\\BC label}
834		& Greater-than unsigned \\[4mm]\hline
835	\end{tabular}
836	\caption{Modifying conditions}\label{tbl:creating-conditions}
837	\end{center}\end{table}
838	shows examples of how these unsupported conditions can be created simply by
839	adjusting the compare instruction, for no extra cost in clocks.
840	Care needs to be taken to ensure that adding one to any immediates, as shown
841	above, does not overflow the size of the immediate field.
842	
843	Many of these alternate conditions are chosen automatically by the ZipCPU
844	compiler.
845	%% }}}
846	\subsection{Operand B}\label{sec:isa-opb}
847	%% {{{
848	Many instruction forms have a 19-bit source ``Operand B'', or OpB for short,
849	associated with them.  This ``Operand B'' is shown in
850	Fig.~\ref{fig:iset-format} as part of the standard instruction format.  For
851	all but the {\tt MOV} instruction, an Operand B is either equal to a register
852	plus a 14--bit signed immediate offset, or an 18--bit signed immediate offset
853	by itself.  This value is encoded as shown in Tbl.~\ref{tbl:opb}.
854	\begin{table}\begin{center}
855	\begin{bytefield}[endianness=big]{19}
856	\bitheader{0-18}  \\
857	\bitbox{1}{0}\bitbox{18}{18-bit Signed Immediate} \\
858	\bitbox{1}{1}\bitbox{4}{Reg}\bitbox{14}{14-bit Signed Immediate}
859	\end{bytefield}
860	\caption{Bit allocation for Operand B}\label{tbl:opb}
861	\end{center}\end{table}
862	This format represents a deviation from many other RISC architectures that use
863	{\tt R0} to represent zero, such as OpenRISC and RISC-V.  Here, instead, we use
864	a bit within the instruction to note whether or not an immediate is used.
865	The result is that ZipCPU instructions can encode larger immediates within
866	their instruction space.
867	
868	In those cases where a fourteen or eighteen bit immediate doesn't make sense,
869	such as for {\tt LDILO}, the extra bits associated with the immediate are
870	simply ignored.  (This rule does not apply to the shift instructions,
871	{\tt ASR}, {\tt LSR}, and {\tt LSL}--which all use all of their immediate bits.)
872	%% }}}
873	\subsection{Address Modes}\label{sec:isa-addr}
874	%% {{{
875	Load and store instructions use the OpB field for their address, whether
876	source or destination.  As a result, the ZipCPU can support both register plus
877	immediate addressing, as well as a limited amount of immediate addressing.
878	%% }}}
879	\subsection{Move Operands}\label{sec:isa-mov}
880	%% {{{
881	The {\tt MOV} instruction is the exception to operand B encoding, with the
882	purpose of providing the supervisor access to user mode registers while in
883	supervisor mode.  The two bits, shown as {\tt A} and {\tt B} in
884	Fig.~\ref{fig:iset-format} above, are designed to contain the high order bit
885	of the 5--bit register index.  If the {\tt B} bit is a `1', the source operand
886	comes from the user register set.  If the {\tt A} bit is a `1', the
887	destination operand is in the user register set.  A zero bit indicates the
888	current register set.
889	
890	This encoding has been chosen to keep the compiler simple.  For the most part,
891	the extra bits are quietly set to zero.  Special assembly instructions,
892	or particular compiler built--in instructions, can be used to get access to
893	these cross register set move instructions.
894	
895	Further, the {\tt MOV} instruction lacks the full OpB capability to use a
896	register or a register plus immediate as a source, since a load immediate
897	instruction, {\tt LDI}, already exists.  As a result, all moves come from a
898	register plus a potential offset.
899	
900	This also creates a situation where a {\tt MOV} instruction may be used
901	like a 3-operand {\tt ADD} instruction.  Because the {\tt MOV} instruction
902	doesn't affect the condition codes, the compiler may use this instruction
903	during address calculation.
904	%% }}}
905	\subsection{Multiply Operations}\label{sec:isa-mpy}
906	%% {{{
907	The ZipCPU supports three separate 32x32-bit multiply instructions: {\tt MPY},
908	{\tt MPYUHI}, and {\tt MPYSHI}.  The first of these produces the low 32-bits
909	of a 32x32-bit multiply result.  The second two produce the upper 32-bits.
910	{\tt MPYUHI} produces the upper 32-bits assuming the multiply was unsigned,
911	whereas {\tt MPYSHI} produces the same bits for signed multiplication.  Each
912	multiply instruction is independent of every other in execution, although
913	the compiler is likely to use them as though they were dependent.
914	
915	In an effort to maintain a fast clock speed, all three of these multiplies
916	have been slowed down in logic.  Thus, depending upon the setting of
917	the {\tt OPT\_MULTIPLY} parameter, the multiply instructions
918	will either 1)~cause an ILLEGAL instruction error ({\tt OPT\_MULTIPLY=0}, or
919	no multiply support), or take {\tt OPT\_MULTIPLY} additional clock
920	cycles to complete.
921	
922	Several multiplication implementations exist, as shown in
923	Tbl.~\ref{tbl:opt-multiply}.
924	\begin{table}\begin{center}
925	\begin{tabular}{c|p{5in}}
926	{\tt OPT\_MULTIPLY} & Implementation\\\hline\hline
927	0 & No multiply support \\\hline
928	1 & Single clock multiply.  This implementation neither registers
929		the inputs to the multiplier, nor the output prior to the ALU
930		output result.\\\hline
931	2 & Two clock multiply.  This implementation registers the inputs to the
932		multiply unit, but the outputs are not registered prior to the
933		ALU mux.\\\hline
934	3 & Three clock multiply.  This implementation registers the inputs to the
935		multiply unit as well as the outputs immediately following the
936		multiply and prior to the final ALU register.  This is the workhorse
937		of most FPGA DSP implementations, although it does require a DSP
938		or DSP combination capable of implementing a 32x32 multiply in a single
939		clock cycle.\\\hline
940	4 & Four clock multiply.  This implementation splits the multiply into
941		multiple sixteen bit multiplies in a FOIL (first, outer, inner, last)
942		binomial multiplication fashion.  The first clock registers the
943		inputs.  The second clock calculates all FOIL values, and the third
944		clock adds the results together to form a full 64-bit multiply result.
945		This method is appropriate for DSP implementations that can handle
946		16x16 bit multiplies but cannot be combined to implement a 32x32 bit
947		multiply.
948		\\\hline
949	5+ & Slow multiply.  This implementation uses (roughly) 32-cycles to achieve
950		a full multiply result.  This is the one implementation that does not
951		use hardware multiply (DSP) support.\\\hline
952	\end{tabular}
953	\caption{Multiply implementation choices}\label{tbl:opt-multiply}%
954	\end{center}\end{table}
955	%% }}}
956	\subsection{Divide Unit}
957	%% {{{
958	The ZipCPU also has an optional divide unit which can be built alongside the
959	ALU.  This divide unit provides the ZipCPU with another two instructions that
960	cannot be executed in a single cycle: {\tt DIVS}, or signed divide, and
961	{\tt DIVU}, the unsigned divide.  These are both 32--bit divide instructions,
962	dividing one 32--bit number by another.  In this case, the Operand B field,
963	whether it be register or register plus immediate, constitutes the denominator,
964	whereas the numerator is given by the other register.

